Could it be a good idea to understand and document how regular =
expressions work and may be used?

1) Consider ages that are good resources to understand regular =
expressions?

	=3D=3D> Consider this one as a good tutorial on regular =
expressions (http://www.regular-expressions.info/tutorial.html)?

=E2=80=9Cthere are 12 characters with special meanings: the backslash \, =
the caret ^, the dollar sign $, the period or dot ., the vertical bar or =
pipe symbol |, the question mark ?, the asterisk or star *, the plus =
sign +, the opening parenthesis (, the closing parenthesis ), the =
opening square bracket [, and the opening curly brace {, These special =
characters are often called "metacharacters". Most of them are errors =
when used alone.=E2=80=9D

		=3D=3D> Shorthand character classes seems good to know =
(http://www.regular-expressions.info/shorthand.html =
<http://www.regular-expressions.info/shorthand.html>)?

		=E2=80=9C\d" is short for [0-9]
		=E2=80=9C\w" stands for "word character". It always =
matches the ASCII characters [A-Za-z0-9_].=20


	=09

2) Consider good online regular expression testers?

	=3D=3D> This seem to be a good one to text javascript regular =
